/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( This is because pear fruit contains sclereids, also known as stone cells, which are a type of sclerenchyma tissue. These cells have thick, lignified cell walls and are scattered throughout the soft tissue of the fruit. Their hardness and rigidity create a gritty and crunchy texture when we chew the fruit.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (\(B\) Branches of a tree move and bend freely in high wind velocity:)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( Tree branches can bend and move freely in the wind due to the presence of collenchyma tissue. Collenchyma cells have unevenly thickened cell walls, which provide both mechanical support and flexibility to the plant. This allows the branches to bend without breaking during strong winds.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (\(C\) Intercellular spaces are absent in sclerenchymatous tissues:)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( In sclerenchyma tissues, the cells are closely packed and have thick, lignified cell walls. These thick walls and tight packing eliminate intercellular spaces, making the tissue rigid and strong. This helps the plant in providing mechanical strength and structural support.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
