/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (47. Sclerenchyma is a type of simple permanent tissue that provides mechanical strength and rigidity to plants. It is made up of dead cells with thick, lignified walls. In stems, sclerenchyma is present to support the plant and help it stand upright, especially in older and mature parts. However, young leaves are still growing and require flexibility for expansion and photosynthesis. The presence of rigid sclerenchyma would hinder their growth and flexibility. Hence, collenchyma and parenchyma are more common in young leaves, while sclerenchyma appears later as the leaf matures.)
/E (47. Sclerenchyma is a type of simple permanent tissue that provides mechanical strength and rigidity to plants. It is made up of dead cells with thick, lignified walls. In stems, sclerenchyma is present to support the plant and help it stand upright, especially in older and mature parts. However, young leaves are still growing and require flexibility for expansion and photosynthesis. The presence of rigid sclerenchyma would hinder their growth and flexibility. Hence, collenchyma and parenchyma are more common in young leaves, while sclerenchyma appears later as the leaf matures.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (48. If the bark of a tree is removed in a circular strip \(a process called girdling or ringing\), it disrupts the phloem tissue located just below the bark. Phloem is responsible for the transport of food \(mainly sugars\) from the leaves to the rest of the plant, including the roots. Without this supply of food, the roots will die, and eventually the entire plant will die due to the failure in food transport. Thus, phloem tissue is the one whose function is affected, and it leads to the death of tissues below the ring.)
/E (48. If the bark of a tree is removed in a circular strip \(a process called girdling or ringing\), it disrupts the phloem tissue located just below the bark. Phloem is responsible for the transport of food \(mainly sugars\) from the leaves to the rest of the plant, including the roots. Without this supply of food, the roots will die, and eventually the entire plant will die due to the failure in food transport. Thus, phloem tissue is the one whose function is affected, and it leads to the death of tissues below the ring.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
